==Combine==
Due to the ongoing [[COVID-19 pandemic]] that started earlier in the year, the invitation-only [[NBA Draft Combine]] and the event's on-court elements were held in multiple phases, lasting from September 28 until November 16, two days before the draft began.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nba.com/article/2020/09/23/nba-announces-format-2020-draft-combine-official-release|title=NBA announces innovative format for Draft Combine 2020|website=[[NBA.com]] |date=September 23, 2020|access-date=September 23, 2020|archive-date=October 2, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201002133013/https://www.nba.com/article/2020/09/23/nba-announces-format-2020-draft-combine-official-release|url-status=live}}</ref> For the first part, players began their league and team interviews via videoconference feeds, similar to this year's draft lottery. This segment lasted from September 28 until October 16. Then, in the second part, players began their individual, on-court programs at the NBA team facility nearest the player's home or interim residence instead of at one standardized area. While players were allowed to continue working out on their own even back in September 2020, this section lasted from October 16 until November 16. This program included strength and agility tests, anthropometric measurements, shooting drills, medical testing and examinations, and a "Pro Day" video filmed via HomeCourt, a mobile basketball training application. The NBA also expanded its Combine HQ tool for this period. While the NBA disallowed their staff members to be involved with these workouts in person (either in the team's practice facilities or near the player himself) at first, they eventually relaxed some of these restrictions to include up to three team executives meeting with a player they're interested in personally, as well as allowed a player to workout with multiple teams at the same time in their towns. The NBA also gave a limit of 10 meetings total with the candidates there, with any extra meetings with someone cutting into their amount allowed for this year.

At the start of this year's draft combine, only 60 prospects were confirmed to participate in this event.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://247sports.com/Article/NBA-Draft-Combine-2020-participants-Udoka-Azubuike-LaMelo-Ball-Vernon-Carey-Devon-Dotson-Ashton-Hagans-Tre-Jones-Payton-Pritchard-Cassius-Winston-152103784/|title=NBA Draft Combine participants revealed|work=247Sports|access-date=September 29, 2020|date=September 27, 2020|archive-date=September 14, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220914175020/https://247sports.com/Article/NBA-Draft-Combine-2020-participants-Udoka-Azubuike-LaMelo-Ball-Vernon-Carey-Devon-Dotson-Ashton-Hagans-Tre-Jones-Payton-Pritchard-Cassius-Winston-152103784/|url-status=live}}</ref> The top, headlining prospect involved with this combine is [[LaMelo Ball]], an automatically eligible draft prospect that gained fame as a professional player both [[Junior Basketball Association|nationally]] and [[BC Prienai|overseas]] years earlier after skipping his junior year of high school and was a top-3 selection for the draft.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nba.com/news/2020-consensus-mock-draft|title=Consensus Mock Draft: Ball leaps Edwards as likely top pick|work=NBA.com|date=October 22, 2020|access-date=November 11, 2020|archive-date=November 11, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201111105037/https://www.nba.com/news/2020-consensus-mock-draft|url-status=live}}</ref> In addition to him, [[R. J. Hampton]] was also invited as an automatically eligible draft prospect for this year, both representing [[National Basketball League (Australia)|Australia's NBL]] as outsider Rising Stars for different teams. Other notable invites include five fully international prospects ([[Deni Avdija]], [[Killian Hayes]], [[Théo Maledon]], [[Paul Eboua]], and [[Karim Mané]], the last of whom played in a Canadian [[CEGEP]]), two high school [[postgraduate year|postgraduates]] ([[Kenyon Martin Jr.]] and [[Josh Hall (basketball)|Josh Hall]]), and [[Jay Scrubb]], a [[junior college]] prospect. Like with prior years, players still held the option to either sit out the combine or have only limited participation there, such as with [[LaMelo Ball]] doing interviews with teams only. For the first half of the combine, each participant was given a standard set of 10 questions to answer in front of each team asking them, as well as league officials under a half-hour set, with players being allowed to interview as many teams as possible. In the second half of the combine, teams were allowed to meet with any candidate that had mutual interest in them back, though each team had a set limit of meetings with players in mind before the draft began. These meetings with players allowed teams to properly gauge each player to the best of their abilities during this time.

==Draft lottery==
{{external media
| float  = right
| video1 = [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QMds6IgnX_8 2020 NBA Draft Lottery Drawing], ''NBA's official YouTube channel''. August 20, 2020.
}}
The [[NBA draft lottery]] is held annually to determine the draft order for the teams that did not make the playoffs in the preceding season. Every NBA team that missed the NBA playoffs holds a chance at winning a top-four pick, but teams with worse records have a better chance at winning a top-four pick, effective as of the [[2019 NBA draft|2019 draft]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/article/2017/09/28/nba-board-governors-approves-changes-draft-lottery-system|title=NBA Board of Governors approves changes to draft lottery system|publisher=National Basketball Association|date=September 28, 2017|access-date=July 25, 2019|archive-date=October 1, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201001141858/https://www.nba.com/article/2017/09/28/nba-board-governors-approves-changes-draft-lottery-system|url-status=live}}</ref> After the lottery selects the teams that receive a top-four pick, the other teams receive an NBA draft pick based on their winning percentage from the prior season. As it is commonplace in the event of identical win-loss records, the NBA performs a random drawing to break ties for not just lottery teams, but also for playoff teams with equal records. This year, the [[Sacramento Kings]] won a tiebreaker for the draft lottery over the [[New Orleans Pelicans]] despite having a better overall record to conclude the regular season, [[2020 NBA Bubble|bubble games]] included.

The lottery was originally scheduled to take place on May 19 at the [[United Center]] in [[Chicago]], [[Illinois]], but was postponed due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]] and the length of the [[Suspension of the 2019–20 NBA season|2019–20 season's suspension]]. On July 21, 2020, the lottery was rescheduled for August 20.<ref name="Lottery">{{cite web|first=Tim|last=Bontemps|title=Memo: NBA moving up draft lottery five days|url=https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/29510755/memo-nba-moving-draft-lottery-five-days|publisher=ESPN|date=July 21, 2020|access-date=July 21, 2020|archive-date=August 29, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200829211535/https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/29510755/memo-nba-moving-draft-lottery-five-days|url-status=live}}</ref> The lottery teams included the eight teams that did not play in the resumed [[2019–20 NBA season]] in July and August: the [[Golden State Warriors]], [[Cleveland Cavaliers]], [[Minnesota Timberwolves]], [[Atlanta Hawks]], [[Detroit Pistons]], [[New York Knicks]], [[Chicago Bulls]], and [[Charlotte Hornets]]. They also included the other six teams that missed the [[2020 NBA playoffs|playoffs]] in the resumed season: the [[Washington Wizards]], [[Phoenix Suns]], [[San Antonio Spurs]], [[Sacramento Kings]], [[New Orleans Pelicans]], and [[Memphis Grizzlies]]<!--by losing to the Portland Trail Blazers in the winner takes all play-in tournament for this season-->, with seeding completely based on the teams' records from March 12, 2020.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://in.nba.com/news/nba-2019-20-season-restart-in-orlando-all-you-need-to-know-from-teams-new-key-dates-schedule-medical-protocols-and-more/1wjyqqo0ndru61t27rk9idm7qn |title=NBA 2019–20 season restart in Orlando: All you need to know from teams, new key dates, schedule, medical protocols and more |publisher=National Basketball Association |date=June 5, 2020 |access-date=August 13, 2020 |archive-date=August 14, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200814084736/https://in.nba.com/news/nba-2019-20-season-restart-in-orlando-all-you-need-to-know-from-teams-new-key-dates-schedule-medical-protocols-and-more/1wjyqqo0ndru61t27rk9idm7qn |url-status=live }}</ref> The new lottery still took place in the United Center, but all guests representing the teams in the lottery attended virtually instead through video communication feeds.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O6wODj7aw2A |title=Minnesota Timberwolves win 2020 NBA Draft lottery &#91; FULL LOTTERY&#93; {{!}} NBA on ESPN |website=[[YouTube]] |access-date=2020-11-18 |archive-date=2020-11-18 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201118210508/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O6wODj7aw2A |url-status=live }}</ref> This year, two of the bottom three teams (Minnesota and Golden State) received the top two selections, while Charlotte and Chicago both jumped up into the top four. Teams that resumed their seasons remained at their initial positions set at the time, with Memphis moving down to the 14th selection after initially being set for a playoff spot.

